From 5f52a8f5cc5846adc5d6b40e60a9343dc45003eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 07:51:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat(validator): note the requests a trace ends without
 answering
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A server that never answers a request, or exits with one pending, produced
`verdict: pass` and nothing else. JSON-RPC expects a response to every
request, but no judgeable clause of either revision states it (`BASE-012` is
excluded), so no verdict is invented: the report gains a note.

`Report::unanswered_requests` (and the multi-revision report's, once) lists
how many requests had no response when the trace ended, the first ten as
method, id and `seq` with who sent them, and whether the trace ended with a
`transport-close`, a `transport-abort` or simply stopped. A request is
answered when the context pairs a response with it — later and out of order
included, and an answer streamed over SSE is a message like any other; a
request its sender cancelled (`notifications/cancelled` naming its id, after
it) is withdrawn and not listed; a request the HTTP layer refused before
reading it (401, 403) was never a message the protocol saw; a request a
server meets with a bare 202 is listed, since nothing answered it.

Every format carries it: a `NOTE` block above the totals in the human report,
the `unanswered_requests` member in JSON (schema updated, additive), a suite
`<property>` in JUnit, a `note`-level invocation notification in SARIF that
leaves `executionSuccessful` alone, and an aside linking each request to its
event in the HTML page. The verdict and exit status do not change.

Two conforming corpus traces pin the note — a `tools/list` left pending at a
`transport-close`, and three requests of which one is answered before the
recording stops — and the 32 existing goldens of traces that end with a
request open now carry it too.

